{"id": "edge-001", "question": "I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST offers a discount fare for riders 65 years and older [doc:mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify a discount for riders aged 62.\n\nTo confirm whether you may qualify for any other reduced-fare program, I'd recommend contacting MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visiting mst.org/fares/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["65"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-001", "is_reference": true, "language": "en", "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST offers a discount fare for riders 65 years and older [mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify a discount for riders aged 62.</p><p>To confirm whether you may qualify for any other reduced-fare program, I'd recommend contacting MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visiting mst.org/fares/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-002", "question": "I'm 62 — can I ride Yolobus at the senior rate?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criteria are: riders aged 62 and older may qualify for Yolobus senior reduced fares. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\nTo use reduced fares, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id] The policy lists acceptable forms as: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nYou may also obtain a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID by visiting:\n- **Yolo Transportation District**, 350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776  \n  Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM  \n  (530) 661-0816\n\nor\n\n- **SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center**, 1225 R St., Sacramento  \n  (916) 321-2877 [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\nSenior fares on Yolobus include: Local $1.00, Intercity $1.00, Express $1.50, and monthly passes at $42.00+. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nTo confirm your eligibility and next steps, contact Yolobus directly at the numbers above.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "BeeLine on-demand microtransit fares are a one-way, flat-fee that is the same for all riders.\nRegular | Seniors (62+)/Disabled & Youth (0-18)\nKnights Landing | $2.00 | $1.50\nWinters | $2.00 | $1.50\nWoodland | $3.00 | $1.50", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["62"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62 — can I ride Yolobus at the senior rate?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criteria are: riders aged 62 and older may qualify for Yolobus senior reduced fares. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>To use reduced fares, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id] The policy lists acceptable forms as: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>You may also obtain a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID by visiting:</p><p>- **Yolo Transportation District**, 350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM</p><p>(530) 661-0816</p><p>or</p><p>- **SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center**, 1225 R St., Sacramento</p><p>(916) 321-2877 [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>Senior fares on Yolobus include: Local $1.00, Intercity $1.00, Express $1.50, and monthly passes at $42.00+. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>To confirm your eligibility and next steps, contact Yolobus directly at the numbers above.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-fares</li><li>yolobus-reduced-fare-id</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-003", "question": "What age counts as a senior on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nAccording to SacRT's published fares, a senior is defined as **age 62 and older** [doc:sacrt-fares]. There is also a separate \"Super Senior\" category for **age 75 and older**, which offers an additional discount on the monthly pass [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nFor questions about eligibility or how to apply for senior fares, contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call their customer service line.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["62"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What age counts as a senior on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>According to SacRT's published fares, a senior is defined as **age 62 and older** [sacrt-fares]. There is also a separate \"Super Senior\" category for **age 75 and older**, which offers an additional discount on the monthly pass [sacrt-fares].</p><p>For questions about eligibility or how to apply for senior fares, contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call their customer service line.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sacrt-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-004", "question": "Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.\n\n**MST:** Discount fares apply to people **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**Yolobus:** Discount fares apply to people **62 years and older** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nSo Yolobus begins its senior discount at age 62, while MST begins at age 65.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["65", "62"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**MST:** Discount fares apply to people **65 years and older** [mst-fares].</p><p>**Yolobus:** Discount fares apply to people **62 years and older** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>So Yolobus begins its senior discount at age 62, while MST begins at age 65.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li><li>yolobus-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-005", "question": "I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior pass for me?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes. SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass** for riders age 75 and older at **$40.00 per month** [doc:sacrt-fares]. This is cheaper than the standard Senior Monthly Pass at $50.00 for ages 62+ [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how you document your age or how to apply for the Super Senior pass. Please contact SacRT directly to confirm the enrollment process and any required documentation.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect ."], "expected_facts": ["75", "$40"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior pass for me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes. SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass** for riders age 75 and older at **$40.00 per month** [sacrt-fares]. This is cheaper than the standard Senior Monthly Pass at $50.00 for ages 62+ [sacrt-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how you document your age or how to apply for the Super Senior pass. Please contact SacRT directly to confirm the enrollment process and any required documentation.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sacrt-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-006", "question": "I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\nNo.\nAccording to Yolobus policy, a Medi-Cal card does **not** qualify for reduced fares.\nTo meet senior/disabled reduced-fare criteria, you must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; **Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal)**; DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency.\n[doc:yolobus-fares]\n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\nFor questions about your specific eligibility, contact Yolobus directly at the number above.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No.</p><p>According to Yolobus policy, a Medi-Cal card does **not** qualify for reduced fares.</p><p>To meet senior/disabled reduced-fare criteria, you must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; **Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal)**; DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency.</p><p>[yolobus-fares]</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>For questions about your specific eligibility, contact Yolobus directly at the number above.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-fares</li><li>yolobus-reduced-fare-id</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-007", "question": "I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criteria state that Medicare Card holders are eligible for a discount fare on MST [doc:mst-fares]. To use the discount, you will need to present your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility when boarding [doc:mst-fares].\n\nYou may also be able to link your discount to a contactless bank card through Cal-ITP Benefits if you have a Medicare account and a contactless-enabled card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express) [doc:mst-fares-benefits].\n\nHowever, the published policy does not specify the exact discount amount or price for Medicare Card holders. For details on the specific fare you would pay, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-007", "is_reference": true, "language": "en", "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criteria state that Medicare Card holders are eligible for a discount fare on MST [mst-fares]. To use the discount, you will need to present your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility when boarding [mst-fares].</p><p>You may also be able to link your discount to a contactless bank card through Cal-ITP Benefits if you have a Medicare account and a contactless-enabled card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express) [mst-fares-benefits].</p><p>However, the published policy does not specify the exact discount amount or price for Medicare Card holders. For details on the specific fare you would pay, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li><li>mst-fares-benefits</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-008", "question": "What proof of service does MST accept for the veteran discount?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST accepts the following proof of service for the veteran discount [doc:mst-fares]:\n\n- DD Form 214\n- DD Form 256N\n- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\n- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card\n- Membership card from the American Legion\n- Disabled American Veterans ID card\n- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation\n\nWith any of these documents, you can obtain an MST Courtesy Card to use for discounted fares [doc:mst-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "To end Veteran homelessness in Monterey County. Each Veteran is paired with a dedicated VTC Case Manager who designs and supervises their recovery program while addressing medical, psychological, occupational, financial, educational, and social issues. They offer several housing programs, including emergency, transitional, and permanent supportive housing.\nhttps://www.vtcofcalifornia.org/programsandservices\nVocational Rehabilitation Specialist Inc\nTo assist veterans in need of housing, job placement, job skill training, and more.\nhttps://vrspecialists.com/", "The Office of Military & Veterans Affairs provides these services and helps with the following benefits:\nComprehensive benefits counseling\nClaims preparation and submission\nClaims follow-up to ensure final decisions are fully adjudicated\nInitiate and develop appeals when appropriate\nNetworking and advocacy with federal, state, and local agencies\nhttps://www.countyofmonterey.gov/government/departments-i-z/military-veterans#mva", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["DD Form 214"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-008", "is_reference": true, "language": "en", "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What proof of service does MST accept for the veteran discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST accepts the following proof of service for the veteran discount [mst-fares]:</p><p>- DD Form 214</p><p>- DD Form 256N</p><p>- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card</p><p>- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card</p><p>- Membership card from the American Legion</p><p>- Disabled American Veterans ID card</p><p>- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation</p><p>With any of these documents, you can obtain an MST Courtesy Card to use for discounted fares [mst-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-009", "question": "Does MST's veteran discount cover my wife too?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST's published discount eligibility includes \"Veteran's spouse/caregiver\" [doc:mst-fares]. However, the policy does not specify what documentation or process is required to establish that relationship or to verify caregiver status.\n\nTo confirm whether your wife qualifies under this category and what proof you'll need to provide, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "To end Veteran homelessness in Monterey County. Each Veteran is paired with a dedicated VTC Case Manager who designs and supervises their recovery program while addressing medical, psychological, occupational, financial, educational, and social issues. They offer several housing programs, including emergency, transitional, and permanent supportive housing.\nhttps://www.vtcofcalifornia.org/programsandservices\nVocational Rehabilitation Specialist Inc\nTo assist veterans in need of housing, job placement, job skill training, and more.\nhttps://vrspecialists.com/", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) offers a taxi voucher program to assist veterans in accessing important locations within their communities.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["spouse"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-009", "is_reference": true, "language": "en", "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does MST's veteran discount cover my wife too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST's published discount eligibility includes \"Veteran's spouse/caregiver\" [mst-fares]. However, the policy does not specify what documentation or process is required to establish that relationship or to verify caregiver status.</p><p>To confirm whether your wife qualifies under this category and what proof you'll need to provide, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-010", "question": "My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYour son rides free. Youth ages 0–18 ride free on Yolobus. [doc:yolobus-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus fixed route bus fares vary depending on which type of service you ride. All below fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026 ."], "expected_facts": ["free"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-010", "is_reference": true, "language": "en", "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Your son rides free. Youth ages 0–18 ride free on Yolobus. [yolobus-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-011", "question": "Is my 17-year-old free on MST like on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo. The policies differ between the two agencies.\n\nOn **Yolobus**, youth ages 0–18 ride free [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nOn **MST**, riders 18 years and under receive a discount fare (not free), and proof of age or an MST Courtesy Card is required upon boarding [doc:mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the exact discounted fare amount for that age group.\n\nTo confirm MST's current discount fare for your 17-year-old, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in summer 2026. Download the free app from the App Store or Google Play.", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes"], "expected_facts": ["18"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is my 17-year-old free on MST like on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No. The policies differ between the two agencies.</p><p>On **Yolobus**, youth ages 0–18 ride free [yolobus-fares].</p><p>On **MST**, riders 18 years and under receive a discount fare (not free), and proof of age or an MST Courtesy Card is required upon boarding [mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the exact discounted fare amount for that age group.</p><p>To confirm MST's current discount fare for your 17-year-old, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li><li>yolobus-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-012", "question": "Do small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes, children under certain conditions ride free on Santa Barbara MTD. Specifically, **children under 45 inches tall ride free, with a limit of three children per adult** [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how height is measured or verified at the farebox.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "La tabla a continuación muestra la actualización de la estructura tarifaria con respecto a la actual. El objetivo de MTD con este ajuste tarifario es recuperar una parte de los aproximadamente $3.0 millones anuales en asistencia operativa del programa FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities, que se perdieron cuando el Censo de 2020 determinó que la población del área urbanizada de Santa Bárbara supera las 200,000 personas. Se estima que el ajuste tarifario generará aproximadamente $1.5 millones en ingresos adicionales anuales.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term."], "expected_facts": ["45 inches"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-012", "is_reference": true, "language": "en", "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, children under certain conditions ride free on Santa Barbara MTD. Specifically, **children under 45 inches tall ride free, with a limit of three children per adult** [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how height is measured or verified at the farebox.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-013", "question": "Can I get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass, and does it work on express routes?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes, Yolobus offers a reduced senior/disabled monthly pass. The senior/disabled monthly pass price is $42.00+ for all three monthly pass types (Local Routes, Intercity Routes, and Express Routes) [doc:yolobus-fares]. \n\nThe senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes — in fact, the published policy notes that \"Senior/disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes\" and \"does not require upgrade\" [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nTo qualify for the senior price, you must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nYou can purchase a monthly pass on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [doc:yolobus-purchasing].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["$42"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass, and does it work on express routes?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, Yolobus offers a reduced senior/disabled monthly pass. The senior/disabled monthly pass price is $42.00+ for all three monthly pass types (Local Routes, Intercity Routes, and Express Routes) [yolobus-fares].</p><p>The senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes — in fact, the published policy notes that \"Senior/disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes\" and \"does not require upgrade\" [yolobus-fares].</p><p>To qualify for the senior price, you must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [yolobus-fares].</p><p>You can purchase a monthly pass on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [yolobus-purchasing].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-fares</li><li>yolobus-purchasing</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-014", "question": "Do UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes. UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding, and they ride for free [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. \n\nUCSB has an agreement with MTD whereby a portion of students' registration fees are provided to MTD, and in exchange students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nHowever, the student ID is valid only during specific periods: from the first day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. Additionally, UCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange", "sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!"], "expected_facts": ["student ID"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes. UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding, and they ride for free [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>UCSB has an agreement with MTD whereby a portion of students' registration fees are provided to MTD, and in exchange students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>However, the student ID is valid only during specific periods: from the first day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter [sbmtd-fares-passes]. Additionally, UCSB &amp; SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-farechange</li><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-015", "question": "Is the bus free for Hartnell College students?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes, according to MST's published policy, Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nTo confirm your eligibility and current semester coverage, contact MST directly or visit mst.org/fares/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nMPC students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nCSUMB students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap."], "expected_facts": ["free"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the bus free for Hartnell College students?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, according to MST's published policy, Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters. [mst-fares]</p><p>To confirm your eligibility and current semester coverage, contact MST directly or visit mst.org/fares/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-016", "question": "I have a disabled rider ID from another transit agency. Will MST honor it?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems [doc:mst-fares]. However, the published policy does not specify whether a \"disabled rider ID\" from another agency qualifies as a courtesy card that MST will accept, or what documentation MST requires to verify that your card is valid.\n\nTo confirm whether your specific ID will be honored, contact MST directly:\n- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)\n- Email or visit an MST customer service location (Bus Stop Shop at 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange at 280 Reservation Road in Marina, or Salinas Transit Center at 110 Salinas Street in Salinas)\n\nFor reference, MST does issue its own free Courtesy Cards to disabled individuals with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card [doc:mst-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a disabled rider ID from another transit agency. Will MST honor it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems [mst-fares]. However, the published policy does not specify whether a \"disabled rider ID\" from another agency qualifies as a courtesy card that MST will accept, or what documentation MST requires to verify that your card is valid.</p><p>To confirm whether your specific ID will be honored, contact MST directly:</p><p>- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)</p><p>- Email or visit an MST customer service location (Bus Stop Shop at 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange at 280 Reservation Road in Marina, or Salinas Transit Center at 110 Salinas Street in Salinas)</p><p>For reference, MST does issue its own free Courtesy Cards to disabled individuals with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card [mst-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-017", "question": "How do I get a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus and what does it require?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\n**How to obtain a reduced-fare photo ID:**\n\nYou may visit either of these locations:\n\n- **Yolo Transportation District**  \n  350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776  \n  Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM  \n  (530) 661-0816\n\n- **SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center**  \n  1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811  \n  Mon–Fri, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM  \n  (916) 321-2877\n\n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\n**What the published policy does not specify:**\n\nThe passages do not state what documentation or proof you must bring to obtain the ID, or what the application process entails. For those details, contact one of the offices above.\n\n**Valid ID for reduced fares:**\n\nOnce you have a reduced-fare ID, the published criteria state that to qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and boarding. Acceptable forms include a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, Medicare Card, California Driver's License, California ID Card, or another transit agency's Disabled ID. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id, doc:yolobus-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811 (Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM)\n(916) 321-2877\nOther forms of IDs that qualify for a discount fare includes:\nMedicare Card\nCalifornia Driver’s License\nCalifornia ID Card\nOther transits agencies’ Disabled ID (such as Unitrans, SacRT, etc.)", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. Fares may be paid on board the bus with cash, but contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app."], "expected_facts": ["Woodland"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How do I get a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus and what does it require?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>**How to obtain a reduced-fare photo ID:**</p><p>You may visit either of these locations:</p><p>- **Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM</p><p>(530) 661-0816</p><p>- **SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center**</p><p>1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811</p><p>Mon–Fri, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM</p><p>(916) 321-2877</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>**What the published policy does not specify:**</p><p>The passages do not state what documentation or proof you must bring to obtain the ID, or what the application process entails. For those details, contact one of the offices above.</p><p>**Valid ID for reduced fares:**</p><p>Once you have a reduced-fare ID, the published criteria state that to qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and boarding. Acceptable forms include a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, Medicare Card, California Driver's License, California ID Card, or another transit agency's Disabled ID. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id, doc:yolobus-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-reduced-fare-id</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-018", "question": "What happens if I lose my SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nIf your SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nFor details on how to obtain a replacement card, contact MTD at 805.963.3366 or visit the MTD Transit Center.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district."], "expected_facts": ["$5"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What happens if I lose my SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>If your SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>For details on how to obtain a replacement card, contact MTD at 805.963.3366 or visit the MTD Transit Center.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-019", "question": "Does my 8-year-old have to pay to ride SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nSacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [doc:sacrt-fares]. Since your 8-year-old would typically be in elementary school (grades TK–12), they may be eligible for this free pass.\n\nHowever, the published policy does not specify the eligibility criteria, documentation requirements, or enrollment process for RydeFreeRT. The passage directs you to check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nI recommend contacting your school district or visiting the RydeFreeRT program directly to confirm your child's eligibility and how to enroll.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00"], "expected_facts": ["free"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 8-year-old have to pay to ride SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [sacrt-fares]. Since your 8-year-old would typically be in elementary school (grades TK–12), they may be eligible for this free pass.</p><p>However, the published policy does not specify the eligibility criteria, documentation requirements, or enrollment process for RydeFreeRT. The passage directs you to check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT [sacrt-fares].</p><p>I recommend contacting your school district or visiting the RydeFreeRT program directly to confirm your child's eligibility and how to enroll.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sacrt-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-020", "question": "My organization wants to buy 25 monthly GoPasses from MST. Is there a bulk discount?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, MST offers a bulk discount for organizations. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nFor an order of 25 monthly GoPasses, you would qualify for the **35% Discount** tier. The discounted monthly GoPass price would be **$45.00 per pass** [doc:mst-fares].\n\n(The 35% discount applies to orders of 21 or more GoPasses; orders of 5–20 GoPasses receive a 25% discount at $52.00 per pass.)\n\nTo enroll in the Group Discount Program, complete the application or call **1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)** [doc:mst-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940."], "expected_facts": ["35%"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My organization wants to buy 25 monthly GoPasses from MST. Is there a bulk discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, MST offers a bulk discount for organizations. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>For an order of 25 monthly GoPasses, you would qualify for the **35% Discount** tier. The discounted monthly GoPass price would be **$45.00 per pass** [mst-fares].</p><p>(The 35% discount applies to orders of 21 or more GoPasses; orders of 5–20 GoPasses receive a 25% discount at $52.00 per pass.)</p><p>To enroll in the Group Discount Program, complete the application or call **1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)** [mst-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-021", "question": "Can I use an SBMTD transfer to come back home later in the day?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo. According to SBMTD policy, \"Transfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips.\" [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nA transfer allows you to board up to two additional buses to complete a single outbound journey, but you cannot use it to return home later. If you need to make a return trip, you would need to pay a new fare.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info."], "expected_facts": ["one-way"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use an SBMTD transfer to come back home later in the day?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No. According to SBMTD policy, \"Transfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips.\" [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>A transfer allows you to board up to two additional buses to complete a single outbound journey, but you cannot use it to return home later. If you need to make a return trip, you would need to pay a new fare.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-022", "question": "I need three buses to get across Santa Barbara — does one transfer cover all of them?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo, one transfer does not cover all three buses. Here's how MTD's transfer policy works:\n\nWhen you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. That transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on your second bus. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nIf you need a third bus, ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer. That second transfer will be valid for 30 minutes. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nSo you will receive two separate transfers to complete your three-bus trip — one after the first bus (good for the second bus) and one after the second bus (good for the third bus). Both transfers are free. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding."], "expected_facts": ["30 minutes"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I need three buses to get across Santa Barbara — does one transfer cover all of them?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No, one transfer does not cover all three buses. Here's how MTD's transfer policy works:</p><p>When you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. That transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on your second bus. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>If you need a third bus, ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer. That second transfer will be valid for 30 minutes. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>So you will receive two separate transfers to complete your three-bus trip — one after the first bus (good for the second bus) and one after the second bus (good for the third bus). Both transfers are free. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-023", "question": "I have a VCTC transfer slip. What do I pay on SBMTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride SBMTD. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value."], "expected_facts": ["$1.00"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a VCTC transfer slip. What do I pay on SBMTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride SBMTD. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-024", "question": "I'm taking the Pacific Surfliner to Santa Barbara — can I ride MTD free with my Amtrak ticket?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, according to the published policy: on the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus, and you can ride MTD for free [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nThis information is based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For details about which specific routes or connections are covered, visit the Pacific Surfliner website or contact MTD at 805.963.3366.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Programas de pases de autobús para estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC: todos los estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC pagan una tarifa de transporte y pueden usar su identificación de estudiante válida como pase de autobús al tocarla en la máquina de tarifas al abordar.\nProgramas de tarifas con descuento: las personas con discapacidades, las personas mayores y los jóvenes son elegibles para tarifas reducidas. También hay descuentos disponibles para pases prepagos, incluidos pases diarios, pases de 10 viajes y pases de 30 días.\nLímite de tarifas : el pago por uso (límite de tarifas) permite a los pasajeros viajar gratis después de haber pagado el equivalente a un pase diario o mensual al usar el pago sin contacto Tap2Ride. El límite de tarifas garantiza que no se le cobrará más de una cantidad específica por día o período de 30 días, sin importar cuántas veces viaje, siempre que pague con la misma tarjeta sin contacto o billetera móvil. Puede encontrar información adicional sobre el límite de tarifas de MTD y Tap2Ride aquí .\nTraslados gratuitos: si necesita usar más de un autobús para llegar a su destino, pídale al conductor un transbordo cuando pague su tarifa en el primer autobús. Los transbordos son gratuitos y se pueden usar como tarifa en hasta dos autobuses más. Los transbordos gratuitos funcionan de la siguiente manera: a pedido, los pasajeros pueden recibir un transbordo gratuito de 60 minutos para usar para abordar un segundo autobús, que se dirija en la misma dirección (es decir, una línea 20 de salida a una línea 6 de salida). Los pasajeros también pueden recibir una transferencia en ese segundo autobús que es válida por 30 minutos, también en la misma dirección. Puede encontrar más información sobre transferencias gratuitas aquí .\nOtras transferencias: ¡Con un comprobante de transferencia válido de VCTC, paga solo $1.00 para viajar en MTD!\nPrograma de transferencia de tránsito de Pacific Surfliner: el día que viaje en el tren Pacific Surfliner, muestre su boleto impreso o electrónico válido de Amtrak Pacific Surfliner al conductor cuando suba al autobús de conexión y viaje en MTD gratis. Visite Pacific Surfliner para obtener más información.\nFuentes de financiamiento alternativas: MTD está buscando activamente nuevas fuentes de financiamiento. MTD continúa solicitando subvenciones para operar y mejorar el servicio, mantener y reemplazar vehículos y mejorar las instalaciones de tránsito en todo el distrito.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["free"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm taking the Pacific Surfliner to Santa Barbara — can I ride MTD free with my Amtrak ticket?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, according to the published policy: on the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus, and you can ride MTD for free [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>This information is based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For details about which specific routes or connections are covered, visit the Pacific Surfliner website or contact MTD at 805.963.3366.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-025", "question": "Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes, you may ride Yolobus with a UC Davis Aggie Card. The published criteria are:\n\n- **UC Davis Aggie Card** is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- The card must be for **Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only** [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nThe published policy does not specify how UC Davis verifies or documents undergraduate or UCDE Global Study status on the card itself. To confirm your card meets these criteria, contact Yolobus Customer Service at the Woodland office (Monday–Thursday, 9am–12pm and 1pm–4pm, excluding holidays) or check with UC Davis.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nAll fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Zip Pass | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid student ID | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Zip Pass: with valid student ID\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app."], "expected_facts": ["Undergrad"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, you may ride Yolobus with a UC Davis Aggie Card. The published criteria are:</p><p>- **UC Davis Aggie Card** is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- The card must be for **Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only** [yolobus-fares]</p><p>The published policy does not specify how UC Davis verifies or documents undergraduate or UCDE Global Study status on the card itself. To confirm your card meets these criteria, contact Yolobus Customer Service at the Woodland office (Monday–Thursday, 9am–12pm and 1pm–4pm, excluding holidays) or check with UC Davis.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-026", "question": "What does Yolobus Special paratransit cost for a local trip versus an intercity one?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\n**Yolobus Special (ADA paratransit) fares:**\n\n- **Local trip:** $4.00 [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- **Intercity trip:** $4.50 [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nThere is also a Premium Fare of $6.00, which applies to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ mile of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare instead. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nFor details on eligibility for ADA paratransit or to purchase tickets, contact Yolobus directly at yolobus.com or call their Customer Service Center in Woodland.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers >", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers ."], "expected_facts": ["$4.00", "$4.50"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does Yolobus Special paratransit cost for a local trip versus an intercity one?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>**Yolobus Special (ADA paratransit) fares:**</p><p>- **Local trip:** $4.00 [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- **Intercity trip:** $4.50 [yolobus-fares]</p><p>There is also a Premium Fare of $6.00, which applies to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ mile of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare instead. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>For details on eligibility for ADA paratransit or to purchase tickets, contact Yolobus directly at yolobus.com or call their Customer Service Center in Woodland.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-027", "question": "I'm in the U.S. on humanitarian parole. Does MST have a discounted fare category for me?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, according to MST's published policy, \"Special Humanitarian Parole\" is listed as a discount fare category [doc:mst-fares].\n\nHowever, the published policy does not specify how to prove humanitarian parole status, what documentation is required, or how to obtain an MST Courtesy Card or other proof of eligibility for this discount.\n\nFor details on how to apply and what documents to bring, please contact MST directly:\n- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)\n- Email or visit an MST customer service location (MST Bus Stop Shop at 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange at 280 Reservation Road in Marina, or Salinas Transit Center at 110 Salinas Street in Salinas)\n\nThis information is based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. Please confirm with MST for the most current requirements.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["Humanitarian Parole"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm in the U.S. on humanitarian parole. Does MST have a discounted fare category for me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, according to MST's published policy, \"Special Humanitarian Parole\" is listed as a discount fare category [mst-fares].</p><p>However, the published policy does not specify how to prove humanitarian parole status, what documentation is required, or how to obtain an MST Courtesy Card or other proof of eligibility for this discount.</p><p>For details on how to apply and what documents to bring, please contact MST directly:</p><p>- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)</p><p>- Email or visit an MST customer service location (MST Bus Stop Shop at 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange at 280 Reservation Road in Marina, or Salinas Transit Center at 110 Salinas Street in Salinas)</p><p>This information is based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. Please confirm with MST for the most current requirements.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>mst-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "edge-028", "question": "Can I get the SBMTD reduced-fare ID with my DMV disabled placard receipt?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, according to the published policy, you may present a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nThe policy also notes that if your photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nFor details about the application process or next steps, you can call MTD at 805.963.3366 or visit the MTD Transit Center. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nThis information is based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. Please confirm with SBMTD for anything time-sensitive.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here ."], "expected_facts": ["Placard"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the SBMTD reduced-fare ID with my DMV disabled placard receipt?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, according to the published policy, you may present a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>The policy also notes that if your photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>For details about the application process or next steps, you can call MTD at 805.963.3366 or visit the MTD Transit Center. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>This information is based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. Please confirm with SBMTD for anything time-sensitive.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-fares-passes</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-001", "question": "How current is your MST fare information?", "provenance": {"source": "Monterey-Salinas Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": false, "expected_facts": ["20\\d\\d"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How current is your MST fare information?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.</p></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-002", "question": "Did SBMTD change its fares recently?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, SBMTD changed its fares recently. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe new fare structure went into effect on **August 18, 2025** [doc:sbmtd-farechange]. The base fare was adjusted from $1.75 to $2.50 [doc:sbmtd-farechange]. This was the first fare change in 16 years; the previous adjustment occurred on January 1, 2009 [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nThe fare increase was made to address rising operating costs. Operating costs had increased by more than 10% since FY 2019, and the District lost approximately $3.0 million per year in Federal Transit Administration (FTA) Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding after the 2020 Census classified Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nNew passes became available on August 4, 2025, and SBMTD offered customers the opportunity to exchange unused or partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, toward the cost of a new pass. Old passes continued to work through August 31, 2025 [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nFor current fares and reduced-fare programs, I recommend confirming directly with SBMTD at sbmtd.gov or contacting them for the most up-to-date information.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "New fare structure for both cash & passes went into effect August 18, 2025. New passes became available on August 4, 2025. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button below.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here ."], "expected_facts": ["August 18, 2025"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Did SBMTD change its fares recently?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, SBMTD changed its fares recently. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The new fare structure went into effect on **August 18, 2025** [sbmtd-farechange]. The base fare was adjusted from $1.75 to $2.50 [sbmtd-farechange]. This was the first fare change in 16 years; the previous adjustment occurred on January 1, 2009 [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>The fare increase was made to address rising operating costs. Operating costs had increased by more than 10% since FY 2019, and the District lost approximately $3.0 million per year in Federal Transit Administration (FTA) Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding after the 2020 Census classified Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>New passes became available on August 4, 2025, and SBMTD offered customers the opportunity to exchange unused or partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, toward the cost of a new pass. Old passes continued to work through August 31, 2025 [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>For current fares and reduced-fare programs, I recommend confirming directly with SBMTD at sbmtd.gov or contacting them for the most up-to-date information.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-farechange</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-003", "question": "Can I still use a ZipPass on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nAccording to SacRT's published notice, **the last day to use passes/tickets is April 30, 2026** [doc:sacrt-fares]. Since that date has passed, ZipPass is no longer available for use on SacRT.\n\nSacRT now offers alternative mobile fare apps: **Transit Connect** and **Tap2Ride** [doc:sacrt-fares]. You can also purchase tickets and passes through fare vending machines at light rail stations or use the **Connect Transit Card** [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nFor the most current information, contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call their customer service line.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here ."], "expected_facts": ["April 30, 2026"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still use a ZipPass on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>According to SacRT's published notice, **the last day to use passes/tickets is April 30, 2026** [sacrt-fares]. Since that date has passed, ZipPass is no longer available for use on SacRT.</p><p>SacRT now offers alternative mobile fare apps: **Transit Connect** and **Tap2Ride** [sacrt-fares]. You can also purchase tickets and passes through fare vending machines at light rail stations or use the **Connect Transit Card** [sacrt-fares].</p><p>For the most current information, contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call their customer service line.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sacrt-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-004", "question": "How long are the current Yolobus fares in effect?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published Yolobus fares are effective **July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nTo confirm whether fares remain current or have changed, contact Yolobus directly at (530) 661-0816 or visit yolobus.com.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. Fares may be paid on board the bus with cash, but contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "Connect Card is the Sacramento region’s electronic transit fare payment system and is accepted by most transit agencies in the Sacramento region. Connect Card readers are installed on all Yolobus vehicles. To pay, tag the Connect Card reader with your card upon boarding the bus.", "Yolobus fixed route bus fares vary depending on which type of service you ride. All below fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026 ."], "expected_facts": ["June 30, 2026"], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How long are the current Yolobus fares in effect?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published Yolobus fares are effective **July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>To confirm whether fares remain current or have changed, contact Yolobus directly at (530) 661-0816 or visit yolobus.com.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>yolobus-fares</li></ul></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-005", "question": "Are the fare prices you quote guaranteed to be what I'll pay tomorrow?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I cannot guarantee that fares will remain the same tomorrow. The passages I have do not state that current fares are locked in or protected from future changes.\n\nWhat I can tell you: The passages document specific fare amounts as of their publication date (for example, SBMTD's base fare was adjusted to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [doc:sbmtd-farechange]), but they do not include any commitment that these prices will not change again.\n\nFor assurance about fare stability or upcoming changes, I recommend contacting your transit agency directly or checking 511.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "sources": ["Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service."], "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Are the fare prices you quote guaranteed to be what I'll pay tomorrow?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I cannot guarantee that fares will remain the same tomorrow. The passages I have do not state that current fares are locked in or protected from future changes.</p><p>What I can tell you: The passages document specific fare amounts as of their publication date (for example, SBMTD's base fare was adjusted to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [sbmtd-farechange]), but they do not include any commitment that these prices will not change again.</p><p>For assurance about fare stability or upcoming changes, I recommend contacting your transit agency directly or checking 511.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>sbmtd-farechange</li></ul></section>"}
